{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "GuntherBiogenicEmissions",
    "description": "WRF-Chem's simple biogenic emission scheme, `bio_emiss_opt = 1` (GUNTHER1): `bio_emissions`, `biogen` and `biosplit` of chem/module_bioemi_simple.F (WRF v4.8.0), after Schoenemeyer, Richter and Simpson et al. (1995) as adapted for MM5 by W. Seidl and for WRF by G. Grell. It needs no input file -- only the surface layer's temperature and pressure, the downward shortwave at the ground and the land-use category -- which is why it is the biogenic scheme the reference configuration can switch on: BEIS3.1.4 is fatal for anything but RADM2/RACM, and MEGAN needs a prepared wrfbiochemi file. It returns the emission of each RADM2 emission class, as WRF's `e_bio` array; turning that into tendencies of a particular mechanism's species is a separate step (GuntherToCBMZ for chem_opt 170).\n\nThe scheme. Each USGS category has standardised emission factors for isoprene, monoterpenes, other VOCs (ug C m-2 h-1) and soil nitrogen (ng N m-2 s-1), with a winter table outside April-September (GuntherParameters), and belongs to one of six land-use classes. The factors are scaled by correction factors for the actual temperature T and light. In the forest classes isoprene follows the Guenther et al. (1993) light and temperature response, C_L C_T with C_L = alpha c_L1 PAR / sqrt(1 + alpha^2 PAR^2), PAR = 2 uE/J times the global radiation, and C_T = exp(C_T1 (T - T_s)/(R T_s T)) / (1 + exp(C_T2 (T - T_M)/(R T_s T))), while monoterpenes and other VOCs follow exp(beta (T - T_s)). In the crop, grassland and other-natural classes every VOC follows exp(0.1 (T - 30 degC)), with no light dependence at all, so a cropland emits isoprene at night. Soil nitrogen follows exp(0.071 T_soil) with a class-specific linear soil temperature. Urban/barren, water and ice emit nothing. Each emission is then converted from a mass flux of carbon (or nitrogen) to a mixing-ratio flux at the surface layer's T/p (ppmv m min-1, the unit biogen writes) and the monoterpene and other-VOC emissions are split over the RADM2 classes by the class's speciation (biosplit); isoprene goes to ISO and nitrogen to NO.\n\nWhat the scheme does that its comments do not say. (1) The vegetation-fraction correction never applies and the winter table is the one selected by `julday`, because `vegflag` is always false (GuntherParameters). (2) The 'other natural' class 6 (shrubland, savanna, wetland, tundra) has a correction factor but no speciation, so its monoterpene and other-VOC emissions are computed and dropped. Both are reproduced. `bio_emissions` also computes a relative humidity, a rain flag, a wet flag and a high-NH3 flag and never uses them; they are omitted.\n\nWhat the tests pin. Steps of the chem170 emissions capture (data/eqwefic/chem170_scm/run_emis.sh, USGS category 2, dryland cropland), and synthetic cases replayed through the same kernel for every land-use class and both seasons, each asserting the emission of every RADM2 class against the real64 replay through chem/module_bioemi_simple.F compiled unchanged (kernels/emissions_driver), at 1e-9 relative.",
    "authors": [
      "EqWeFiC"
    ],
    "created": "2026-11-12T00:00:00Z",
    "tags": [
      "wrf-chem",
      "emissions",
      "biogenic",
      "gunther",
      "chem_opt-170"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "WRF-Chem v4.8.0, chem/module_bioemi_simple.F (subroutines bio_emissions, bioemiin, biogen, biosplit), called from chem/emissions_driver.F for bio_emiss_opt = 1."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Simpson, D., Guenther, A., Hewitt, C. N. and Steinbrecher, R. (1995). Biogenic emissions in Europe 1. Estimates and uncertainties. J. Geophys. Res., 100(D11), 22875-22890."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Guenther, A. B., Zimmerman, P. R., Harley, P. C., Monson, R. K. and Fall, R. (1993). Isoprene and monoterpene emission rate variability: model evaluations and sensitivity analyses. J. Geophys. Res., 98(D7), 12609-12617."
      }
    ]
  },
